"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_069","cnm_058","cnm_066","cnm_059"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"1","reason":"The base planes ABFE and DCGH are parallel. Their intersections with the front lateral plane are AB and DC, so AB is parallel to DC.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h119_q2","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_066"],"id":"m2","label":"Right angles in a lateral rectangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m2":["cnm_186","cnm_068"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_186","cnm_068","cnm_066"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"90","reason":"Let angle AEF be α. Then angle EFD is 180°-α, so the angles of triangle EGF at E and F sum to α/2+(180°-α)/2=90°. Therefore angle G is 90°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_070_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_066","cnm_059"],"id":"m1","label":"Bisected angles and the triangle angle sum","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_069","cnm_058","cnm_p124"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_069","cnm_058","cnm_p124","cnm_066","cnm_059"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"2","reason":"Statements ① and ③ are true. Statement ② has counterexamples, and ④ is not universally true without a parallelism condition. Thus there are two true propositions.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_071_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_063","cnm_066","cnm_069","cnm_068"],"id":"m1","label":"Check each proposition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_071"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_071","cnm_063","cnm_066","cnm_069","cnm_068"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: When two lines are parallel, alternate interior angles are equal","reason":"With the line through A parallel to BC, transversals AB and AC give angles at A equal to angles B and C. Together with angle BAC, they form a straight angle.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_115_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_066","cnm_067","cnm_056"],"id":"cnm_115_q4_m1","label":"Transfer angles using a parallel line","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cnm_115_q4_m1":["cnm_069","cnm_115"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_069","cnm_115","cnm_066","cnm_067","cnm_056"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (100°)","reason":"AB ∥ CD gives ∠ABC = 180° - 110° = 70°, so ∠ABD = 70° - 30° = 40°. Since AD = BD, ∠DAB = 40°, and ∠ADB = 180° - 80° = 100°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1981_AMC_12_Q05","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_066","cnm_067","cnm_110"],"id":"parallel-isosceles-angles","label":"Transfer an angle into the isosceles triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"parallel-isosceles-angles":["cnm_069","cnm_137","cnm_115"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_069","cnm_137","cnm_115","cnm_066","cnm_067","cnm_110"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (30)","reason":"Since MN is parallel to BC, the bisector conditions give MB = MO and NC = NO. Consequently MN = MB + NC, and the perimeter is (AB - MB) + (MB + NC) + (AC - NC) = AB + AC = 30.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1982_AMC_12_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_058","cnm_066","cnm_067"],"id":"1982_AMC_12_Q10_m1","label":"Find two isosceles triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"1982_AMC_12_Q10_m1":["cnm_069","cnm_138","cnm_055"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_069","cnm_138","cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_066","cnm_067"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 30","reason":"Let I be the incenter. Since MN is parallel to BC, the angle bisectors give BM = MI and CN = NI. Thus the perimeter is AM + AN + MN = (AB − BM) + (AC − CN) + MI + IN = AB + AC = 30.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_12A_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_114","cnm_066"],"id":"cancel-equal-segments","label":"Cancel equal segments","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cancel-equal-segments":["cnm_264","cnm_069","cnm_138","cnm_055"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_264","cnm_069","cnm_138","cnm_055","cnm_114","cnm_066"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: a+b","reason":"Let X be on AB with DX parallel to BC. Parallelogram XBCD gives XB=CD=b. The parallel-line angle relations and angle D=2 angle B give angle ADX=angle AXD=angle B, so AX=AD=a. Thus AB=a+b.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1970_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_066","cnm_067"],"id":"auxiliary-parallelogram","label":"Split the trapezoid into a triangle and a parallelogram","methodDirectNodeIds":{"auxiliary-parallelogram":["cnm_069","cnm_138","cnm_184","cnm_183"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_069","cnm_138","cnm_184","cnm_183","cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_066","cnm_067"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_062","cnm_066","cnm_067"],"id":"extend-to-isosceles-triangles","label":"Extend the legs to form two isosceles triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"extend-to-isosceles-triangles":["cnm_069","cnm_115","cnm_138"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_069","cnm_115","cnm_138","cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_062","cnm_066","cnm_067"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E (95°)","reason":"Triangle BED gives ∠BED = ∠BDE = 45°, so ∠AED = 40° + 45° = 85°. Thus ∠CDE = 360° - 90° - 90° - 85° = 95°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1995_AMC_8_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_056","cnm_p101","cnm_066","cnm_067"],"id":"parallel-sides","label":"Use the parallel sides","methodDirectNodeIds":{"parallel-sides":["cnm_058","cnm_p124","cnm_068","cnm_069"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_058","cnm_p124","cnm_068","cnm_069","cnm_056","cnm_p101","cnm_066","cnm_067"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":9,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. BC is parallel to MN.","reason":"The angle bisector theorem gives BD/CD=AB/AC. Since BM=BD and CN=CD, BM/AB=CN/AC, hence AM/AB=AN/AC. With the common included angle A, triangles AMN and ABC are similar, so MN∥BC.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1975_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_114","cnm_268","cnm_111","cnm_270","cnm_066"],"id":"angle-bisector-and-similarity","label":"Angle bisector ratios and similarity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"angle-bisector-and-similarity":["math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_068"]},"nodeIds":["math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_068","cnm_250","cnm_114","cnm_268","cnm_111","cnm_270","cnm_066"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":9,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 4/5","reason":"Extend AB and CD to meet at X beyond A and D. In triangle BDX, the exterior angle BDC gives angle BXD = 46° - 23° = 23°, so XD = BD = 1. Since triangles XAD and XBC are similar, XD/XC = AD/BC = 5/9. Hence CD = XC - XD = 9/5 - 1 = 4/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2009_AMC_12B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_p210","cnm_066","cnm_067"],"id":"solution:extend-trapezoid-legs:a24dceab75cc","label":"Extend the legs to form similar triangles","